    "compose_patient": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "Your child came to hospital because she was very unwell. For about 2 weeks she had been drinking a lot, passing a lot of urine, and losing weight. She then started vomiting, had tummy pain, and became drowsy.\n\nThe team found she had diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). This is when the body does not have enough insulin and harmful substances called ketones build up in the blood. This was also the first sign that your child has type 1 diabetes."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team ran blood tests. These showed high blood sugar, high ketones, and that her blood had become too acidic. The team started treatment straight away following the DKA protocol. This included:\n\n- Fluids given through a drip\n- Insulin given through a drip\n- Potassium replacement through the drip\n- Close monitoring every hour\n\nThe intensive care team (PICU) was kept informed. Your child was cared for in the high-dependency area of the ward.\n\nBy Day 2, her blood tests were improving. She was moved on to insulin given by injection under the skin (a basal-bolus regimen).\n\nThe diabetes nurse and dietician began working with your family. Education sessions covered carbohydrate counting, how to manage low blood sugar (a hypo), and what to do when your child is unwell (sick-day rules)."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "- The diabetes team will see your child in 1 week.\n- A dietician follow-up has been arranged.\n- Phone support from the diabetes team is available in the meantime.\n- Your GP has been told about your child's admission. No urgent action is needed from them at this stage."
        },
